# The machine looking at itself, and this collection looking at
# itself.  Some of these meet os9exec's limits rather than their
# own -- where they do, the screen says so.

shot    about
size    30 80
cap     The collection's own guide: `about hack' answers what a program is,
cap     where it came from, what it opens and whether its source survived --
cap     out of the four files the web catalogue is built from, read at the
cap     moment you ask, so it cannot drift from what shipped.
run     about hack
wait    5
shot    aprocs
cap     A process monitor -- and it cannot run here.  It reads the system
cap     globals through F$SetSys, which os9exec does not implement, and
cap     aborts.  This is a limit of the emulator, not of the program.
run     aprocs
wait    5
kill
shot    argproc_demo
cap     argproc_demo PARSING A COMMAND LINE, which is the whole point of it:
cap     RICO's argproc() takes the switches, fills in the defaults for the
cap     ones you left out, and hands back the filename.  Switches take their
cap     arguments with NO SPACE -- `-x99', never `-x 99'.  It wanted STACK.
cap     Rebuilt with MEM=64k from the source in SRC/argproc and it runs --
cap     the recipe carries the flag now.
run     argproc_demo readme
wait    6
run     argproc_demo -x99 -sHello readme
wait    6
run     argproc_demo -help
wait    8
shot    bigsetter
cap     A Modula-2 set-operations demonstration -- proof the Modula
cap     compiler's output runs here.
run     bigsetter
wait    8
shot    bootgen
cap     bootgen builds an OS9Boot file for a machine to start from -- you
cap     name the device and the modules to put in it.  There is no boot
cap     file to build here, so it answers with its command format.
run     bootgen -?
wait    8
shot    btree
cap     A B-tree demonstration, building and walking an index.
run     btree
wait    8
shot    clock
cap     clock displays a running clock, and cannot here: it opens a pipe
cap     to feed its display and answers `Popen error 216' when that pipe
cap     cannot be made.
run     clock; echo "clock returned $?"
wait    6
shot    config
cap     config reports this machine's C type properties as the #defines a
cap     portable program would want.
run     config
wait    5
shot    cpu
cap     A CPU speed test.  It takes os9exec down with it every time -- the
cap     emulator exits mid-run, which is why the screen stops where it does.
run     cpu
wait    10
shot    dam
cap     The disk allocation map: one character per cluster across a 253 MB
cap     volume, so you can see where the collection sits on it.
run     dam /dd | head -n 21
wait    10
shot    date
cap     date prints the date and time.  It reads the year as 2100, which is
cap     why the disk carries the setyear and fixyear repairs; `date -t'
cap     shows the same clock in the format setime prompts for.
run     date
wait    4
run     date -t
wait    4
shot    demo
cap     demo, the egetopt option-parsing example, showing how it read the
cap     command line it was given.
run     demo -a -b -c=7 one two
wait    6
shot    devprc
for     devprc ptxminst t_trtest
cap     devprc shows which device belongs to which process.  Rebuilt here:
cap     the archived module had been patched and would not run.  `ptxminst'
cap     installs Ptxm, and `t_trtest' exercises a trap handler.
run     devprc -h
wait    8
run     ptxminst -?
wait    8
run     t_trtest
wait    8
shot    dinfo
cap     dinfo reports a device's geometry and free space -- here the volume
cap     the collection is mounted as: its size, sector size and how much of
cap     it is in use.
run     dinfo /dd
wait    5
shot    dload
cap     Loads a data file INTO A DATA MODULE in memory, despite the name --
cap     it does not send anything down a serial line.  Given the message of
cap     the day it reports the size it read and the address the module was
cap     placed at.
run     clear
run     dload /dd/SYS/motd
wait    7

shot    drop
size    30 80
cap     drop puts back exactly what keep wrote.  A program is kept first,
cap     then dropped: drop reads the receipt in SYS/kept and removes each
cap     file, refusing any whose checksum has changed since it was kept, so
cap     your saves and scores are safe from it.
run     rm -f /dd/SYS/kept; rm -f /dd/tmp/SYSB/kp/CMDS/greg > /nil 2>/nil; keep -p /dd/tmp/SYSB/kp greg > /nil
wait    5
run     clear
wait    1
run     drop greg
wait    5
shot    every
cap     every runs a command again and again at a fixed interval:
cap     `every 2 oskversion' asks the kernel its version every two seconds
cap     until you stop it.  The first two answers are on the screen.
run     every 2 oskversion
wait    14
kill
shot    fastcc
for     fastcc lfmaker
cap     fastcc, a quicker driver for cc, and lfmaker.
run     fastcc -?
wait    6
run     lfmaker < /nil
wait    10
shot    flink
cap     flink makes a second directory entry for a file, under a name you
cap     give -- an RBF hard link.  It is shown by its usage: RBF has no true
cap     hard links, and removing such an entry can leave the original
cap     pointing at the wrong place, so it is not run on the disk here.
run     flink -?
wait    5
shot    gen
size    30 80
cap     gen writes the FRAME of a new C program -- header block, authorship
cap     and version lines and sectioned comments.  It appends `.c' to the
cap     name you give, which is why it looks as though it wrote nothing:
cap     `gen -p demo' leaves demo.c, whose head is shown here.
run     rm -f /dd/tmp/SYSB/demo.c > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/SYSB > /nil 2>/nil
wait    3
run     clear
wait    1
run     gen -p /dd/tmp/SYSB/demo
wait    5
run     cat /dd/tmp/SYSB/demo.c | head -n 16
wait    6
shot    getsys
cap     getsys reports OS-9's system globals, reading the global page
cap     field by field -- the count of forked processes, the memory
cap     limits, the priority range and the system debugger's pointers.
run     getsys | head -n 21
wait    6
shot    hinterhalt
for     hinterhalt testibc sqrtx bigsetter i_am_i
cap     hinterhalt, a small game that came with EFFO forum 7 -- one of the
cap     example programs the German OS-9 user group circulated.  Four more
cap     from the same shelf are asked for beside it -- `testibc' exercises
cap     the inter-process bus, `sqrtx' takes a square root, `bigsetter'
cap     demonstrates Modula-2 set operations and `i_am_i' reports its own
cap     process.
run     hinterhalt
wait    10
run     testibc
wait    8
run     sqrtx 144
wait    8
run     bigsetter
wait    8
run     i_am_i
wait    8
shot    i_am_i
cap     i_am_i prints its own source: a Pascal quine.
run     i_am_i
wait    5
shot    ident
for     liborder.os9
cap     liborder.os9 lists the object modules inside an OS-9 library and the
cap     order they are linked in.  version, mexist, modinfo and names each
cap     ask their own question about a module and have cards of their own.
run     liborder.os9 -?
wait    6
shot    isam
cap     The indexed-sequential file demonstration from the same shelf.
run     isam
wait    8
shot    keep
size    30 80
cap     keep takes a program off the collection onto your own /dd, copying
cap     the binary and the files DOC/DEPENDS says it reads.  Here greg goes
cap     into a scratch directory, and keep names each file it wrote and
cap     records it so drop can put it back.
run     rm -f /dd/SYS/kept; rm -f /dd/tmp/SYSB/kp/CMDS/greg > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/SYSB > /nil 2>/nil
wait    3
run     clear
wait    1
run     keep -p /dd/tmp/SYSB/kp greg
wait    6
shot    kept
size    30 80
cap     kept lists what keep has taken from the collection and what it came
cap     to.  A program is kept first, then listed: each file, its size, and
cap     the total.
run     rm -f /dd/SYS/kept; rm -f /dd/tmp/SYSB/kp/CMDS/greg > /nil 2>/nil; keep -p /dd/tmp/SYSB/kp greg > /nil
wait    5
run     clear
wait    1
run     kept
wait    5
shot    launch
cap     launch is a login helper: it reads SYS/config, sets the environment
cap     for your terminal type -- and optionally a default PATH and the emacs
cap     bindings -- then starts the shell you name.  It answers a bare -?
cap     with its command format.
run     launch -?
wait    6
shot    makecrc
cap     makecrc generates C source for six CRC polynomials, one file each,
cap     and says nothing -- the files it leaves are the output.  Run in a
cap     writable directory, then listed.
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/UTILA/mc > /nil 2>/nil
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/mc > /nil 2>/nil
run     clear
wait    1
run     ksh -c "cd /dd/tmp/UTILA/mc; makecrc" 2>/nil
wait    4
run     ls /dd/tmp/UTILA/mc
wait    3
shot    map
cap     map shows the blocks a file occupies -- not a memory map, whatever
cap     the name suggests.
run     map -e /dd/DOC/INDEX
wait    5
shot    modinfo
cap     An OS-9 module header read back: name, type, size, edition and CRC.
cap     Every program on this disk is a module like this one.
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/today
wait    3
shot    names
cap     names lists the modules inside a file. os9lib is the FORTRAN
cap     runtime, and it holds many.
run     names /dd/CMDS/os9lib
wait    5
kill
shot    ob68kdemo
cap     OmniBasic 1.16, a BASIC compiler, in its demonstration build.
run     ob68kdemo
wait    8
shot    oskversion
cap     oskversion prints the OS-9 version the system reports -- the number,
cap     the CPU it thinks it is on, and the edition.
run     oskversion
wait    3
shot    passwd
cap     passwd changes a login password in SYS/password.  It matches on the
cap     user name, and the file it edits is plain text you can read here --
cap     the card shows the file, then passwd's command format.
run     cat /dd/SYS/password
wait    5
run     passwd -?
wait    6
shot    phone
size    24 80
cap     `phone' connects two terminals over a communication path, so you
cap     can type to somebody on another terminal.  Give it one -- `phone
cap     /t1' -- and it draws its face and starts ringing: OS9/68K PHONE
cap     FACILITY, the terminal it is calling, the try count.  Control-E
cap     leaves.  With no path it prints its syntax.  The tail of the bottom
cap     line is the echoed command that was on that row when phone painted
cap     over it.
run     phone /t1
wait    28
shot    scsiutil
for     scsiutil expreserve
cap     scsiutil talks to a SCSI device; expreserve is vi's crash
cap     recovery, preserving an edit buffer when the editor dies.  Neither
cap     has anything to work on here, and each answers with its usage.
run     scsiutil -?
wait    8
run     expreserve -?
wait    8
shot    vi-recovery
size    40 80
for     dedit exrecover read_mail
cap     `read_mail' READING A FOLDER, and the vi recovery helper beside it.
cap     read_mail is not vi's helper despite sitting among them: it is a
cap     small mail reader with a mail directory of its own, /dd/MAIL, and it
cap     quotes what it finds and offers to delete it.  `exrecover' brings
cap     back a buffer that `expreserve' saved when vi died; asked for one
cap     nobody saved it says `File not found', which is the honest answer on
cap     a disk where vi has not crashed.  `dedit' is
cap     Carl Kreider's BASIC09 disk sector editor and needs runb, like the
cap     other three BASIC09 programs here.  The folder is cut to seven lines
cap     so the whole exchange fits on one screen. read_mail must be given
cap     its answer ON STANDARD INPUT -- `N' from a file returns, and so does
cap     `n', while read_mail with NO input at all re-prompts without bound
cap     and fills a capture with four megabytes of the same question.
run     exrecover -r /dd/tmp/nothing
wait    8
run     head -n 7 /dd/SPOOL/MAIL/tester > /dd/MAIL/mail_tester
wait    5
run     echo "N" > /dd/tmp/rm.in
wait    4
run     read_mail < /dd/tmp/rm.in
wait    10
shot    sddemo
cap     White's Speedisk 2.10, a disk defragmenter, drawing the map of a
cap     volume it is about to work on.
run     sddemo
wait    10
shot    shdev
cap     shdev lists the system's device table -- what is mounted and the
cap     driver behind each one.  It prints its heading and no rows: there is
cap     no OS-9 device table here for it to walk.  dinfo asks a named device
cap     directly and does answer; it has its own card.
run     shdev
wait    6
shot    spline
cap     spline fits a curve through points and writes PostScript -- the
cap     `%!' at the head of the line is the PostScript magic.  It writes
cap     the same stream whatever it is given, including -?, so it never
cap     tells you what it wants.
run     echo "0 0" > /dd/tmp/pts
wait    2
run     spline /dd/tmp/pts | head -n 14
wait    5
shot    sqrtx
cap     A square-root demonstration -- one of the small programs the forums
cap     circulated to prove a compiler worked.
run     sqrtx
wait    6
shot    ssl
cap     ssl asks RBF for a file's segment list -- the sectors it was given,
cap     in order.  It prints the heading and no rows for every file tried
cap     here: os9exec's RBF does not hand back the file descriptor sector,
cap     so there is nothing for ssl to list.  `map -e' answers the same
cap     question and does work; it has its own card.
run     ssl /dd/DOC/INDEX
wait    4
shot    sysid
cap     sysid asks the kernel to identify itself through F$SysID.  That call
cap     is not implemented here, so the line that comes back reports it as
cap     unimplemented -- which is what sysid has to show on this system.
run     sysid
wait    5
shot    sysmon
size    28 80
cap     sysmon draws memory and process state.  It asks whether to create
cap     SYS/nodedef, times out on the keyboard, and draws its Process
cap     Monitor -- the task table, the two percentage rules and the CPU it
cap     thinks it is on -- before it takes a bus error at F$GPrDsc, the
cap     get-process-descriptor call this system does not answer.  dinfo, map
cap     and space answer the questions it would have and have cards of their
cap     own.
run     sysmon < /nil
wait    45
shot    top
cap     top lists processes by their share of the CPU.  It prints its column
cap     headings and then stops at F$GPrDBT, the process-table call this
cap     system does not answer; aprocs, its neighbour, stops the same way.
run     top
wait    6
shot    tty
for     tty vecho lmargin
cap     tty names the terminal; vecho writes without a newline; lmargin
cap     sets a left margin on what follows.
run     tty
wait    5
run     vecho "no newline here -- "
wait    5
run     lmargin -?
wait    6
shot    ub68kdemo
cap     UniBasic 1.10, a BASIC compiler, in its demonstration build for the
cap     68000 -- its opening banner, which names the processor it was built
cap     for.  The symbol table is capped in the demo; nothing else is.
run     ub68kdemo | head -n 3
wait    8
shot    ub68020demo
cap     The same UniBasic 1.10 demonstration built for the 68020 -- the one
cap     line that differs is the processor it announces.
run     ub68020demo | head -n 3
wait    8
shot    vc
for     vc
cap     `vc' is a SPREADSHEET -- "Welcome to the Spreadsheet Calculator,
cap     type ? for help" -- with rows, columns and a formula line.  `?' is
cap     the help key and `q' quits; it does not answer Ctrl-E, so this card
cap     leaves it the way the program expects.
run     vc
wait    8
quit    q\r
shot    version
cap     version prints a module's own edition number -- `version <module>'
cap     reads the edition byte from the module header, whatever the file is
cap     named.
run     version /dd/CMDS/today
wait    4
shot    yagi
cap     yagi designs a Yagi antenna to DL6WU's method: give it a frequency,
cap     an element count, a boom diameter and a tube size, and it works out
cap     the lengths and spacings.  144 MHz, nine elements, 20 mm boom.
run     yagi
wait    4
send    144\r
wait    3
send    9\r
wait    4
send    20\r
wait    4
send    N\r
wait    5
send    4\r
wait    10
shot    modules
for     cio csl csl020 math math881 os9lib rtfdat
cap     The runtime modules the starred programs need.  They are not
cap     programs -- you cannot run them -- so this is what they look like to
cap     `modinfo': name, type, edition and CRC. cio and math ship here by
cap     Microware's permission; SOURCES.txt records the exchange.  All seven
cap     are asked for below, one line each
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/cio | head -n 6
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/csl | grep -a "name\|Edition\|size"
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/csl020 | grep -a "name\|Edition\|size"
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/math | grep -a "name\|Edition\|size"
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/math881 | grep -a "name\|Edition\|size"
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/os9lib | grep -a "name\|Edition\|size"
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/rtfdat | grep -a "name\|Edition\|size"
wait    12
shot    drivers
for     keydrv.mm1 msdrv.901_340 msdrv_340.901.ms rb37c65 scsi_mm1a snddrv windio.52
cap     The MM/1 drivers -- keyboard, mouse, floppy, SCSI, sound and the
cap     windowing terminal.  They are device drivers for hardware that is
cap     not here, so `modinfo' is what there is to show: type $C (device
cap     driver) rather than $1 (program).  Each is asked for, one line apiece.
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/MM1/keydrv.mm1 | head -n 4
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/MM1/msdrv.901_340 | head -n 4
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/MM1/rb37c65 | head -n 4
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/MM1/scsi_mm1a | head -n 4
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/MM1/snddrv | head -n 4
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/MM1/windio.52 | head -n 4
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/MM1/msdrv_340.901.ms | head -n 4
wait    12
shot    load
cap     load puts a MODULE in the module directory so that a program which
cap     LINKS it can find it.  `for', the RTF Fortran driver, prints nothing
cap     at all until os9lib is loaded -- and then it says what it takes.  It
cap     is called by its full path because `for' is a bash keyword and the
cap     shell would answer first.  That silence is the whole symptom, and
cap     this is the cure: a clean-room load, contributed by the os9exec
cap     project and built here, with its source in SRC/load.
run     /dd/CMDS/for
wait    6
run     load /dd/CMDS/os9lib
wait    8
run     /dd/CMDS/for
wait    8
run     load /dd/CMDS/nosuchthing
wait    8
shot    modules2
for     ptxm wn.stb
cap     Two more modules that are not programs: `ptxm', Nick Holgate's Path
cap     Table eXtension Module, which `ptxminst' installs, and `wn.stb', the
cap     symbol table that ships beside the WN web server.
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/ptxm | head -n 12
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/NETWORK/wn.stb | head -n 4
wait    10
shot    mtst
for     mtst
cap     mtst exercises the C maths library and prints what each function
cap     returned beside what it was given -- ceil, floor and round on the
cap     same number, integer and floating side by side.  It is one of the
cap     small programs a port was checked with.
run     mtst | head -n 16
wait    12
shot    fontgen
for     fontgen
cap     fontgen writes the SOURCE of a character font: an OS-9 data module
cap     in assembler, psect and all, ready for the 80-column card it was
cap     written for.  It reads its bitmap from standard input and will sit
cap     waiting on the terminal if you forget to give it one.
run     fontgen < /nil | head -n 10
wait    12
shot    dpark
cap     dpark restores an RBF disk's head to track 00 -- what you did before
cap     moving a drive.  With no disk head to park here it answers with its
cap     command format.
run     dpark -?
wait    5
shot    bsplt68
cap     bsplt68 takes an OS9Boot file apart into the modules inside it.
cap     There is no boot file here, so it answers with its usage line.
run     bsplt68
wait    5
shot    signal
cap     signal sends an OS-9 signal to a process, after a delay if you ask
cap     for one.  With no process named it prints its syntax: the process
cap     id, the signal code and the seconds to wait.
run     signal -?
wait    5
shot    sysmax
cap     sysmax reports the system's maximum process age -- the scheduler
cap     setting that bounds how far a waiting process's priority can climb.
cap     The setting is not kept here, so it reads back as zero.
run     sysmax
wait    5
shot    sysmin
cap     sysmin reports the system's minimum process priority, the floor the
cap     scheduler ages processes up from.  The setting is not kept here, so
cap     it reads back as zero.
run     sysmin
wait    5
shot    t
cap     t is a minimal trap-handler test stub: it installs, returns cleanly
cap     and prints nothing, so a zero exit status is the whole of what it
cap     has to show.
run     t; echo "status $?"
wait    4
shot    mexist
cap     mexist tests whether a MODULE is in memory and sets its exit status
cap     -- zero when the module is there, one when it is not.  bash is
cap     resident; nosuchmodule is not.
run     mexist bash; echo "status $?"
wait    4
run     mexist nosuchmodule; echo "status $?"
wait    4
shot    vis
size    24 80
cap     vis runs a command over and over and redraws the screen with its
cap     output, the way `watch' does elsewhere.  Its header names the
cap     command, the delay between runs and the number of runs so far.
run     vis -d=1 date
wait    4
send    \023
wait    1
snap
send    \021
send    \003
wait    2
shot    repeat
size    24 80
cap     repeat runs a command a number of times -- `repeat 2 date' runs date
cap     twice.  It hands the line to $SHELL, which SYS/login sets to ksh;
cap     date writes no trailing newline, so the two runs abut.
run     repeat 2 date
wait    4
shot    cron
cap     cron is a scheduling daemon: it reads SYS/crontab, then forks each
cap     command at its minute, hour and day and runs until it is stopped.
cap     It is not run here -- there is no screen for a daemon that waits --
cap     so the card shows the crontab it would act on.
run     cat /dd/usr/lib/crontab
wait    5
shot    setime
cap     setime sets the system clock.  It prompts with the field order it
cap     wants, YYMMDDHHMMSS -- year, month, day, hour, minute, second.
run     setime -?
wait    5
shot    adduser
size    30 80
cap     adduser adds a login to the system, or removes one with -r.  With no
cap     name given it prints its usage: the options and the command forms it
cap     accepts.
run     adduser -?
wait    6
shot    combine
cap     combine interleaves two files BYTE BY BYTE -- the even bytes from
cap     one, the odd bytes from the other -- which is how a 16-bit EPROM
cap     image is rebuilt from its two 8-bit halves.  Eight A's and eight
cap     B's go in; the interleaved result comes out.
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/UTILA/cmb > /nil 2>/nil
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/cmb > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'AAAAAAAA' > /dd/tmp/UTILA/cmb/even
run     printf 'BBBBBBBB' > /dd/tmp/UTILA/cmb/odd
run     clear
wait    1
run     combine /dd/tmp/UTILA/cmb/even /dd/tmp/UTILA/cmb/odd /dd/tmp/UTILA/cmb/mix
wait    3
run     cat /dd/tmp/UTILA/cmb/mix; echo
wait    3
shot    demerge
cap     demerge splits a file of several OS-9 modules back into one file
cap     per module.  OS-9's `merge' is concatenation, so `cat' makes the
cap     input: today and tty joined into one file, then taken apart into
cap     today and tty again.
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/UTILA/dm > /nil 2>/nil
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/dm > /nil 2>/nil
run     cat /dd/CMDS/today /dd/CMDS/tty > /dd/tmp/UTILA/dm/both
run     clear
wait    1
run     ksh -c "cd /dd/tmp/UTILA/dm; demerge both" 2>/nil
wait    4
run     ls -l /dd/tmp/UTILA/dm
wait    3
shot    ptob
cap     ptob turns a file of bit patterns back into characters, the other
cap     half of `btop': `Hi' becomes rows of dots and O's, and ptob reads
cap     those back to `Hi'.
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/pb > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'Hi' > /dd/tmp/UTILA/pb/t
run     btop /dd/tmp/UTILA/pb/t > /dd/tmp/UTILA/pb/bits
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat /dd/tmp/UTILA/pb/bits
wait    2
run     ptob /dd/tmp/UTILA/pb/bits > /dd/tmp/UTILA/pb/back
wait    2
run     cat /dd/tmp/UTILA/pb/back; echo
wait    2
shot    trunc
cap     trunc cuts a file to a given length: `trunc <path> <num>'.  A
cap     sixteen-byte file is cut to eight, and the listing either side of
cap     it is the whole demonstration.
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/tr > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf '0123456789ABCDEF' > /dd/tmp/UTILA/tr/f
run     clear
wait    1
run     ls -l /dd/tmp/UTILA/tr/f
wait    2
run     trunc /dd/tmp/UTILA/tr/f 8
wait    2
run     ls -l /dd/tmp/UTILA/tr/f; cat /dd/tmp/UTILA/tr/f; echo
wait    3
shot    add_errmsg
cap     add_errmsg appends error messages to vi's message file,
cap     /dd/SYS/vi_errmsg, one per quoted argument.  It seeks to the end
cap     and writes, and the file grows by the message.
run     clear
wait    1
run     wc -c /dd/SYS/vi_errmsg
wait    2
run     add_errmsg "disk full -- delete something"
wait    3
run     wc -c /dd/SYS/vi_errmsg
wait    2
shot    clear
cap     clear clears the terminal screen.  A directory listing is printed
cap     and then wiped by clear -- only the line typed after it is left.
run     dir /dd/CMDS
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     echo "the listing above was wiped by clear"
wait    2
shot    lgrep
cap     lgrep lists the files a pattern appears in; its banner claims the
cap     same result as `grep -l'.  It prints nothing even for a string
cap     that is present, so `grep -l' is the one to reach for.
cap     DOC/README-GREP compares the searchers.
run     lgrep -?
wait    3
run     lgrep ksh /dd/SYS/password; echo "found nothing, status $?"
wait    3
shot    suse
cap     suse is meant to print a program's usage line.  It prints nothing
cap     for any module tried, by name or by full path, and returns
cap     success.
run     suse cat; echo "status $?"
wait    3
run     suse /dd/CMDS/today; echo "status $?"
wait    3
shot    pri
cap     pri changes a process's priority: `pri <pid> <priority>'.  There
cap     is no process listing on this disk to show the change, and it
cap     reports nothing whether the process exists or not.
run     pri 2 128; echo "status $?"
wait    3
shot    snd_sig
cap     snd_sig sends a signal to one or more processes named by pid;
cap     `-(n)' is the signal number, and with none it sends the wake
cap     signal.  Its synopsis, there being no process here to signal.
run     snd_sig -?
wait    3
shot    setime2
cap     setime2 sets the system date and time from the fields
cap     `yyy mm dd hh mm ss' at its prompt -- yyy counts years from 1900,
cap     so 126 is 2026 -- and prints the date it set.
run     clear
wait    1
run     setime2
wait    2
send    126 9 3 14 30 0\r
wait    3
shot    setyear
cap     setyear sets the year directly and prints the date it made:
cap     `setyear 2026'.
run     setyear 2026
wait    3
shot    suspend
cap     suspend REMOVES a process from the system, as its own Function
cap     line says, rather than pausing it; `-s' reaches system programs
cap     too.  Its syntax, there being no process here to remove.
run     suspend
wait    3
shot    umusek
cap     umusek is a music editor.  It wants a hardware screen address and
cap     stops without one: `DS_ScAdd Error 208', then `Can't get screen
cap     addr, bye'.
run     umusek; echo "status $?"
wait    5
shot    unpacklib.os9
cap     unpacklib extracts the object modules from an OS-9 library into
cap     separate .r files.  Run on the C library it writes them into the
cap     current directory, stopping at the first record that is not a
cap     module.
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/UTILA/ul > /nil 2>/nil
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/ul > /nil 2>/nil
run     clear
wait    1
run     ksh -c "cd /dd/tmp/UTILA/ul; unpacklib /dd/LIB/alib.l" 2>/nil
wait    5
run     ls /dd/tmp/UTILA/ul
wait    3
shot    ynad
size    24 80
cap     YNAD -- Yet Another Name & Address program, a full-screen contact
cap     database.  Its title bar on opening; there is no address file yet.
run     ynad
wait    8
snap
send    q
wait    2
shot    bootlogger
cap     bootlogger records what happens during system start-up to a log.
cap     Run by hand, outside boot, it has nothing to record and returns
cap     silently.
run     bootlogger; echo "status $?"
wait    3
shot    screen_nocio
cap     screen_nocio is the trap-free build of `screen', a login greeting
cap     that picks a file at random from $HOME/.SCREENS and RUNS it
cap     through the shell.  It reaches the lookup where the cio build will
cap     not start; running the greeting needs Microware's shell, and
cap     without one the child is aborted.
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/UTILA/home > /nil 2>/nil
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/home/.screens > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'today\r' > /dd/tmp/UTILA/home/.screens/hi
run     export HOME=/dd/tmp/UTILA/home
run     clear
wait    1
run     /dd/CMDS/REBUILT/screen_nocio; echo "st $?"
wait    5
shot    break
cap     break sends a BREAK on a serial line, an assembler example from
cap     the forums.  Run bare it calls the system-debugger trap and, with
cap     no debugger attached, does not return; asked with `-?' it exits at
cap     once.  Asked for by path, because bash has a break of its own.
run     /dd/CMDS/break -?; echo "returned $?"
wait    4
shot    transfer
cap     transfer copies files from GDOS DISKS to OS-9 and takes no options
cap     -- not a general device-to-device copier; `cp' and `dsave' are
cap     those.  It wants the GDOS device DGDOS0, which is not present.
run     transfer
wait    4
send    \r
wait    2
shot    preset
cap     preset programs the terminal's function keys with a fixed set of
cap     definitions -- dir, umacs, r68, l68, dsave and so on.  It takes no
cap     arguments and ignores any given.
run     preset
wait    4
shot    vlen
cap     vlen is a variable-length-record demonstration: it builds a small
cap     filesystem of its own, writes a hundred records of differing
cap     length, and reads them back deleting some as it goes.  The tail of
cap     the run, ending `vlTest completed successfully'.
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/UTILA/vl > /nil 2>/nil
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UTILA/vl > /nil 2>/nil
run     clear
wait    1
run     ksh -c "cd /dd/tmp/UTILA/vl; vlen" 2>/nil | tail -l=16
wait    6
shot    mvolformat
cap     mvolformat formats a set of floppies with sequential volume
cap     numbers.  With no drive here, its syntax and options are what
cap     there is to show.
run     mvolformat
wait    3
shot    rndir
cap     rndir converts the NAMES of the subdirectories in a directory to
cap     upper or lower case; `-l' makes them lower case, `-q' works
cap     quietly.  Its options.
run     rndir -?
wait    4
